module

public import Mathlib.AlgebraicGeometry.IdealSheaf.Subscheme
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.Ideal.AssociatedPrime.Basic
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.Support.RelativeAssassinValuationRing

/-!
# Associated primes across the stalk of a scheme-theoretic fibre

Let `f : X ⟶ Y` be a morphism of schemes, `y : Y` and `p` a point of the scheme-theoretic fibre
`f.fiber y` with image `x = f.fiberι y p` in `X`.
`StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Morphisms.Support.RelativeAssassinValuationRing` identifies the
local ring of the fibre with a quotient of the local ring of `X`,
`𝒪_{X_y,p} = 𝒪_{X,x}/𝔪_y·𝒪_{X,x}`, and defines the associated points of a sheaf of modules
(Tag 02OJ) and the relative assassin (Tag 05AT) in terms of associated primes of a stalk.

Being a relative associated point is a statement about associated primes **over the fibre's local
ring**; every commutative-algebra input to it — notably
`Stacks.ValuationRingFibre.maximalIdeal_mem_associatedPrimes_quotient`, the valuation-ring core
of Stacks 0GSI — is a statement about associated primes **over the local ring of `X`**, because
that is the ring the module of sections is a module over. This file closes that mismatch: along
a surjective ring map the two notions of associated prime agree under `Ideal.comap`, and the
stalk map of a fibre inclusion is such a map and carries the maximal ideal to the maximal ideal.

The second half of the file does the same for the other closed-immersion-like map the lemma 0GSI
needs, the inclusion of the closed subscheme cut out by an ideal sheaf: it computes the stalk of
`Scheme.IdealSheafData.subscheme`, giving `𝒪_{V(I),z} = 𝒪_{X,x}/I_x`, where `I_x` is the stalk of
the ideal sheaf. Mathlib describes `I` only by its values `I.ideal U` on *affine* opens, and
`Scheme.IdealSheafData.ker_subschemeι_app` only on a single affine open at a time; passing to the
stalk is where those local data are glued, and it is done here.

## Main results

* `Stacks.FibreStalk.isAssociatedPrime_comap_iff`: let `π : B ⟶ C` be a surjective ring
  homomorphism and let `M` be a module over both `B` and `C` with `b • m = π b • m`. Then an
  ideal `p` of `C` is an associated prime of `M` over `C` if and only if `p.comap π` is an
  associated prime of `M` over `B`. Neither ring is assumed Noetherian and no finiteness is
  assumed of `M`.
* `Stacks.FibreStalk.comap_maximalIdeal_of_isLocalHom`: a local homomorphism of local rings pulls
  the maximal ideal back to the maximal ideal. (Surjectivity is not needed.)
* `Stacks.FibreStalk.mem_associatedPrimes_maximalIdeal_stalkMap_fiberι_iff`: the bridge, for the
  stalk map `𝒪_{X,x} ⟶ 𝒪_{X_y,p}` of a fibre inclusion — `𝔪_{X_y,p}` is an associated prime of
  a module over `𝒪_{X_y,p}` if and only if `𝔪_{X,x}` is an associated prime of that same module
  over `𝒪_{X,x}`.
* `Stacks.FibreStalk.mem_associatedPrimes_maximalIdeal_toFibreStalk_iff`: the same bridge for the
  projection `𝒪_{X,x} ⟶ 𝒪_{X,x}/𝔪_y·𝒪_{X,x}`, which is the shape in which
  `Stacks.ValuationRingFibre.maximalIdeal_mem_associatedPrimes_quotient` delivers its conclusion.

## Main definitions

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.stalkIdeal`: the stalk `I_x ⊆ 𝒪_{X,x}` of an ideal
  sheaf at a point — the ideal generated by the germs at `x` of the sections of `I` over the
  affine opens containing `x`.

and, for it:

* `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.ker_stalkMap_subschemeι`: **the stalk of the closed
  subscheme cut out by an ideal sheaf.** The stalk map of `I.subschemeι` at `z` has kernel exactly
  `I.stalkIdeal (I.subschemeι z)`. Since that stalk map is surjective (`I.subschemeι` is a
  preimmersion) this says `𝒪_{V(I),z} = 𝒪_{X,x}/I_x`, packaged as
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.subschemeStalkRingEquiv` and pinned down by
  `AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData.subschemeStalkRingEquiv_toSubschemeStalk`.
* `Stacks.FibreStalk.mem_associatedPrimes_maximalIdeal_stalkMap_subschemeι_iff`: the
  associated-prime bridge for that stalk map.

## Implementation notes

The compatibility of the two scalar actions is carried as a plain hypothesis
`∀ b m, b • m = π b • m` rather than as `[Algebra B C] [IsScalarTower B C M]`. This is not
cosmetic. In the geometric application the `B`-action on the stalk is obtained from the
`C`-action by `RingHom.toAlgebra` on the stalk map, inside a `letI`, so there is no `Algebra`
instance in the ambient context to state a tower against; and the module structure the fibre's
stalk carries comes from Mathlib's `PresheafOfModules` stalk instance, which is not an
`IsScalarTower` for any `Algebra` instance declared here. A hypothesis in this form applies to
whatever pair of actions the consumer has, and is discharged by `rfl` whenever the `B`-action is
literally restriction of scalars along `π`.

The surjectivity of `π` is used twice and both uses are essential: to transport primality from
`p.comap π` back to `p`, and — through `Ideal.comap_injective_of_surjective` — to recover `p`
itself from the computation of `p.comap π`. Without it only the easy direction survives, which is
the direction appearing as an inlined `have` in the proof of
`Stacks.ValuationRingFibre.maximalIdeal_mem_associatedPrimes_quotient`.

Nothing is narrowed: the ring-level results are universe-polymorphic in `B`, `C` and `M`
independently, and the geometric results are stated for an arbitrary morphism of `Scheme.{u}`
and an arbitrary point of an arbitrary scheme-theoretic fibre, exactly as in
`RelativeAssassinValuationRing`. `stalkIdeal` and `ker_stalkMap_subschemeι` are stated for an
arbitrary `Scheme.IdealSheafData`, not only for the vanishing ideal of a closure, so that the
reduced-closure case used in the valuation-ring argument is an instance rather than the theorem.

`stalkIdeal` is an `iSup` over the affine opens containing `x` of `Ideal.map` of the germ, not
the `Ideal.map` of a single `I.ideal U` for one chosen `U`: no single affine open contains the
germ of every element, and no coherence between two affine opens is available from Mathlib's
`IdealSheafData` beyond the localization condition `map_ideal_basicOpen`. The `iSup` is what makes
both inclusions of `ker_stalkMap_subschemeι` provable from one-affine-open data.

The inclusion `ker ⊆ I_x` is the one with content, and it is proved by shrinking twice. A germ is
first written over an *affine* open by `TopCat.Presheaf.exists_mem_germ_eq_of_isBasis` at
`Scheme.isBasis_affineOpens`, so that `ker_subschemeι_app` applies at all. Its image then dies on
*some* open `W` of the subscheme, by `TopCat.Presheaf.germ_eq`; `W` need not be a preimage of
anything a priori, and this is where `IsPreimmersion I.subschemeι` is spent — `subschemeι` is an
embedding, so `Topology.IsInducing.isOpen_iff` presents `W` as `I.subschemeι ⁻¹ᵁ V''`. A second
shrink inside `U ⊓ V''`, again to an affine open, lands in a single affine chart on which
`ker_subschemeι_app` can be read. Both restriction maps involved agree because hom-sets in
`Opens` are subsingletons, which is why `Subsingleton.elim` appears rather than a `simp` over
`homOfLE` composites.

Two near-neighbours in the draft, neither of which is this:

* `Stacks.RingedSpace.stalkIdeal` is the stalk of an ideal *subsheaf* of the structure sheaf of a
  ringed space, `Ideal.span` of the germs of its sections over *all* opens. `stalkIdeal` here is
  for Mathlib's `Scheme.IdealSheafData`, whose data is an ideal on each *affine* open only
  (`Scheme.IdealSheafData.ideal : ∀ U : X.affineOpens, Ideal Γ(X, U)`), with no sheaf of ideals
  attached and no conversion to one in Mathlib. The two are therefore different objects over
  different inputs and the earlier one does not apply; if a conversion is ever built they should
  be compared.
* `SpreadingOut.comap_maximalIdeal_of_isLocalHom`
  (`StacksFrontier/RingTheory/SpreadingOut/ResidueFibre.lean`) is literally the same statement as
  `comap_maximalIdeal_of_isLocalHom` below. It is not imported here on purpose: its module sits on
  top of the non-Noetherian local flatness criterion and pulls in some twelve hundred further
  modules, which is the wrong dependency for a file about fibre stalks to acquire for a
  four-line fact about local rings. The duplication is deliberate and is flagged so it can be
  removed by hoisting the lemma into a shared low-level module (or upstreaming it), not by adding
  that import.

This file supplies no comparison of *module* stalks. Turning
`Scheme.Modules.IsAssociatedPoint ((Scheme.Modules.pullback (f.fiberι y)).obj F) p` into a
statement about the stalk `F_x` additionally needs the stalk of a pullback of sheaves of
modules, `(g^*F)_p ≅ F_{g p} ⊗_{𝒪_{X,g p}} 𝒪_{Z,p}`, for Mathlib's `SheafOfModules.pullback`;
that is a separate bridge and is not available (see the references below for what the draft does
have).

## References

* [Stacks Project, Tag 0GSI](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0GSI)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 02OJ](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/02OJ)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 05AT](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/05AT)
* [Stacks Project, Tag 0098](https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0098)
-/

@[expose] public section

open AlgebraicGeometry CategoryTheory IsLocalRing Submodule

universe u

namespace Stacks.FibreStalk

section Ring

variable {B C : Type*} [CommRing B] [CommRing C] (π : B →+* C)
  {M : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module B M] [Module C M]

/-- The annihilator of an element computed over `B` is the pullback along `π` of the annihilator
computed over `C`, as soon as the `B`-action factors through `π`. -/
theorem comap_colon_bot (hsmul : ∀ (b : B) (m : M), b • m = π b • m) (x : M) :
    ((⊥ : Submodule C M).colon {x}).comap π = (⊥ : Submodule B M).colon {x} := by
  ext b
  simp only [Ideal.mem_comap, Submodule.mem_colon_singleton, Submodule.mem_bot, hsmul]

/-- An ideal whose pullback along a surjection is prime is itself prime. -/
theorem isPrime_of_isPrime_comap (hs : Function.Surjective π) {p : Ideal C}
    (h : (p.comap π).IsPrime) : p.IsPrime where
  ne_top' htop := h.ne_top (by simp [htop])
  mem_or_mem' {a b} hab := by
    obtain ⟨a, rfl⟩ := hs a
    obtain ⟨b, rfl⟩ := hs b
    exact h.mem_or_mem (by simpa only [Ideal.mem_comap, map_mul] using hab)

/-- **Associated primes transfer across a surjection.** Let `π : B →+* C` be surjective and let
`M` be a module over both `B` and `C`, with the `B`-action factoring through `π`. Then an ideal
`p` of `C` is an associated prime of `M` over `C` exactly when `p.comap π` is an associated prime
of `M` over `B`.

Neither ring is assumed Noetherian and `M` is not assumed finite: the radical in the definition
of `IsAssociatedPrime` commutes with `Ideal.comap`, which is what makes the two sides match
verbatim. -/
theorem isAssociatedPrime_comap_iff (hs : Function.Surjective π)
    (hsmul : ∀ (b : B) (m : M), b • m = π b • m) (p : Ideal C) :
    IsAssociatedPrime (p.comap π) M ↔ IsAssociatedPrime p M := by
  have key : ∀ x : M, (((⊥ : Submodule C M).colon {x}).radical).comap π
      = ((⊥ : Submodule B M).colon {x}).radical := fun x ↦ by
    rw [Ideal.comap_radical, comap_colon_bot π hsmul x]
  constructor
  · rintro ⟨hprime, x, hx⟩
    exact ⟨isPrime_of_isPrime_comap π hs hprime, x,
      Ideal.comap_injective_of_surjective π hs (hx.trans (key x).symm)⟩
  · rintro ⟨hprime, x, rfl⟩
    exact ⟨Ideal.comap_isPrime π _, x, key x⟩

/-- Membership form of `isAssociatedPrime_comap_iff`. -/
theorem mem_associatedPrimes_comap_iff (hs : Function.Surjective π)
    (hsmul : ∀ (b : B) (m : M), b • m = π b • m) (p : Ideal C) :
    p.comap π ∈ associatedPrimes B M ↔ p ∈ associatedPrimes C M :=
  isAssociatedPrime_comap_iff π hs hsmul p

/-- A local homomorphism of local rings pulls the maximal ideal back to the maximal ideal: an
element of the source is a nonunit exactly when its image is. -/
theorem comap_maximalIdeal_of_isLocalHom [IsLocalRing B] [IsLocalRing C] [IsLocalHom π] :
    (maximalIdeal C).comap π = maximalIdeal B := by
  ext b
  simp only [Ideal.mem_comap, IsLocalRing.mem_maximalIdeal, mem_nonunits_iff]
  exact not_congr ⟨fun h ↦ isUnit_of_map_unit π b h, fun h ↦ h.map π⟩

/-- **The maximal ideal transfers across a surjective local homomorphism.** For `M` a module over
both `B` and `C` with the `B`-action factoring through a surjective local homomorphism
`π : B →+* C` of local rings, `𝔪_C` is an associated prime of `M` over `C` exactly when `𝔪_B` is
an associated prime of `M` over `B`. -/
theorem mem_associatedPrimes_maximalIdeal_iff [IsLocalRing B] [IsLocalRing C] [IsLocalHom π]
    (hs : Function.Surjective π) (hsmul : ∀ (b : B) (m : M), b • m = π b • m) :
    maximalIdeal B ∈ associatedPrimes B M ↔ maximalIdeal C ∈ associatedPrimes C M := by
  rw [← comap_maximalIdeal_of_isLocalHom π]
  exact mem_associatedPrimes_comap_iff π hs hsmul _

end Ring

section Scheme

variable {X Y : Scheme.{u}} (f : X ⟶ Y)

/-- **The bridge at the stalk of a scheme-theoretic fibre.** Let `p` be a point of `f.fiber y`
with image `x = f.fiberι y p`, and let `M` be a module over the local ring `𝒪_{X_y,p}` of the
fibre which is also a module over `𝒪_{X,x}` through the stalk map of `f.fiberι y`. Then `𝔪_{X_y,p}`
is an associated prime of `M` over `𝒪_{X_y,p}` exactly when `𝔪_{X,x}` is an associated prime of
`M` over `𝒪_{X,x}`.

The stalk map of `f.fiberι y` is surjective because `f.fiberι y` is a preimmersion, and it is a
local homomorphism because every stalk map of a morphism of schemes is. This is the form in which
the condition defining a relative associated point (Tag 05AT, through Tag 02OJ) becomes a
statement of commutative algebra over `𝒪_{X,x}`. -/
theorem mem_associatedPrimes_maximalIdeal_stalkMap_fiberι_iff (y : Y) (p : f.fiber y)
    {M : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module (X.presheaf.stalk (f.fiberι y p)) M]
    [Module ((f.fiber y).presheaf.stalk p) M]
    (hsmul : ∀ (b : X.presheaf.stalk (f.fiberι y p)) (m : M),
      b • m = ((f.fiberι y).stalkMap p).hom b • m) :
    maximalIdeal (X.presheaf.stalk (f.fiberι y p))
        ∈ associatedPrimes (X.presheaf.stalk (f.fiberι y p)) M ↔
      maximalIdeal ((f.fiber y).presheaf.stalk p)
        ∈ associatedPrimes ((f.fiber y).presheaf.stalk p) M :=
  mem_associatedPrimes_maximalIdeal_iff ((f.fiberι y).stalkMap p).hom
    ((f.fiberι y).stalkMap_surjective p) hsmul

/-- **The bridge in quotient form.** The same statement for the projection
`𝒪_{X,x} ⟶ 𝒪_{X,x}/𝔪_{f x}·𝒪_{X,x}` of
`AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.Hom.toFibreStalk`: for `M` a module over the quotient which is also a
module over `𝒪_{X,x}` through the projection, `𝔪` of the quotient is an associated prime of `M`
there exactly when `𝔪_{X,x}` is an associated prime of `M` over `𝒪_{X,x}`.

This is the shape in which `Stacks.ValuationRingFibre.maximalIdeal_mem_associatedPrimes_quotient`
states its conclusion — associated primes of `N/𝔪_A N` over `B`, not over `B/𝔪_A B` — so this
lemma is what converts that conclusion into the condition on the fibre. -/
theorem mem_associatedPrimes_maximalIdeal_toFibreStalk_iff (x : X)
    {M : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module (X.presheaf.stalk x) M] [Module (f.fibreStalk x) M]
    (hsmul : ∀ (b : X.presheaf.stalk x) (m : M), b • m = (f.toFibreStalk x).hom b • m) :
    maximalIdeal (X.presheaf.stalk x) ∈ associatedPrimes (X.presheaf.stalk x) M ↔
      maximalIdeal (f.fibreStalk x) ∈ associatedPrimes (f.fibreStalk x) M :=
  mem_associatedPrimes_maximalIdeal_iff (f.toFibreStalk x).hom
    Ideal.Quotient.mk_surjective hsmul

end Scheme

end Stacks.FibreStalk

namespace AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData

open TopologicalSpace Opposite

variable {X : Scheme.{u}} (I : X.IdealSheafData)

/-- **The stalk of an ideal sheaf.** The ideal of `𝒪_{X,x}` generated by the germs at `x` of the
sections of `I` over the affine opens containing `x`. This is the ideal written `I_x` when one
says `𝒪_{V(I),x} = 𝒪_{X,x}/I_x`.

Mathlib's `Scheme.IdealSheafData` records an ideal `I.ideal U ⊆ Γ(X, U)` for each *affine* open
`U`, so the supremum is taken over those; it is not the image of any single one. -/
noncomputable def stalkIdeal (x : X) : Ideal (X.presheaf.stalk x) :=
  ⨆ U : {U : X.affineOpens // x ∈ U.1.1},
    Ideal.map (X.presheaf.germ U.1.1 x U.2).hom (I.ideal U.1)

/-- The germ at `x` of a section of `I` over an affine open containing `x` lies in `I_x`. -/
theorem germ_mem_stalkIdeal {x : X} (U : X.affineOpens) (hx : x ∈ U.1)
    {a : Γ(X, U.1)} (ha : a ∈ I.ideal U) :
    (X.presheaf.germ U.1 x hx).hom a ∈ I.stalkIdeal x :=
  le_iSup (fun U : {U : X.affineOpens // x ∈ U.1.1} ↦
    Ideal.map (X.presheaf.germ U.1.1 x U.2).hom (I.ideal U.1)) ⟨U, hx⟩
      (Ideal.mem_map_of_mem _ ha)

/-- The germs of sections of `I` die in the subscheme cut out by `I`. This is the easy half of
`ker_stalkMap_subschemeι`, and it is `ker_subschemeι_app` one affine open at a time. -/
theorem stalkIdeal_le_ker_stalkMap_subschemeι (z : I.subscheme) :
    I.stalkIdeal (I.subschemeι z) ≤ RingHom.ker ((I.subschemeι).stalkMap z).hom := by
  refine iSup_le fun U ↦ Ideal.map_le_iff_le_comap.2 fun a ha ↦ ?_
  simp only [Ideal.mem_comap, RingHom.mem_ker]
  rw [Scheme.Hom.germ_stalkMap_apply,
    RingHom.mem_ker.1 ((I.ker_subschemeι_app U.1).ge ha), map_zero]

/-- **The stalk of the closed subscheme cut out by an ideal sheaf.** For a point `z` of
`I.subscheme` with image `x = I.subschemeι z` in `X`, the stalk map `𝒪_{X,x} ⟶ 𝒪_{V(I),z}` has
kernel exactly the stalk `I_x` of the ideal sheaf. Since that stalk map is surjective
(`I.subschemeι` is a preimmersion), this says `𝒪_{V(I),z} = 𝒪_{X,x}/I_x`. -/
theorem ker_stalkMap_subschemeι (z : I.subscheme) :
    RingHom.ker ((I.subschemeι).stalkMap z).hom = I.stalkIdeal (I.subschemeι z) := by
  refine le_antisymm (fun s hs ↦ ?_) (I.stalkIdeal_le_ker_stalkMap_subschemeι z)
  rw [RingHom.mem_ker] at hs
  obtain ⟨U, hxU, hU, a, rfl⟩ :=
    TopCat.Presheaf.exists_mem_germ_eq_of_isBasis X.isBasis_affineOpens X.presheaf
      (I.subschemeι z) s
  rw [Scheme.Hom.germ_stalkMap_apply] at hs
  have hs' : (I.subscheme.presheaf.germ (I.subschemeι ⁻¹ᵁ U) z hxU)
      ((Scheme.Hom.app I.subschemeι U).hom a)
      = (I.subscheme.presheaf.germ (I.subschemeι ⁻¹ᵁ U) z hxU) 0 := by
    rwa [map_zero]
  obtain ⟨W, hzW, iU, iV, hW⟩ := I.subscheme.presheaf.germ_eq z hxU hxU _ _ hs'
  obtain ⟨t, ht, hpre⟩ := (I.subschemeι.isEmbedding.isInducing.isOpen_iff).1 W.2
  set V'' : X.Opens := ⟨t, ht⟩
  have hWV : W = I.subschemeι ⁻¹ᵁ V'' := TopologicalSpace.Opens.ext hpre.symm
  have hxV'' : I.subschemeι z ∈ V'' := show z ∈ I.subschemeι ⁻¹ᵁ V'' from hWV ▸ hzW
  obtain ⟨-, ⟨V, hV, rfl⟩, hxV, hVsub⟩ :=
    X.isBasis_affineOpens.exists_subset_of_mem_open
      (show I.subschemeι z ∈ (U ⊓ V'' : X.Opens) from ⟨hxU, hxV''⟩) (U ⊓ V'').2
  have hVU : V ≤ U := hVsub.trans inf_le_left
  have hVV : V ≤ V'' := hVsub.trans inf_le_right
  have h1 : I.subschemeι ⁻¹ᵁ V ≤ W := hWV ▸ I.subschemeι.preimage_mono hVV
  have hpreVU := I.subschemeι.preimage_mono hVU
  have key : (I.subscheme.presheaf.map (homOfLE hpreVU).op)
      ((Scheme.Hom.app I.subschemeι U).hom a) = 0 := by
    have e1 : (homOfLE hpreVU).op = iU.op ≫ (homOfLE h1).op :=
      Quiver.Hom.unop_inj (Subsingleton.elim _ _)
    rw [e1, Functor.map_comp, ConcreteCategory.comp_apply, hW, ← ConcreteCategory.comp_apply,
      map_zero]
  have e2' : X.presheaf.map (homOfLE hVU).op ≫ Scheme.Hom.app I.subschemeι V
      = Scheme.Hom.app I.subschemeι U ≫ I.subscheme.presheaf.map (homOfLE hpreVU).op := by
    rw [Scheme.Hom.naturality]
    congr 1
  have e2 : (Scheme.Hom.app I.subschemeι V).hom ((X.presheaf.map (homOfLE hVU).op) a)
      = (I.subscheme.presheaf.map (homOfLE hpreVU).op)
        ((Scheme.Hom.app I.subschemeι U).hom a) :=
    DFunLike.congr_fun (CommRingCat.hom_ext_iff.mp e2') a
  have hmem : (X.presheaf.map (homOfLE hVU).op) a ∈ I.ideal ⟨V, hV⟩ := by
    rw [← I.ker_subschemeι_app ⟨V, hV⟩, RingHom.mem_ker, e2, key]
  rw [(X.presheaf.germ_res_apply' (homOfLE hVU).op (I.subschemeι z) hxV a).symm]
  exact I.germ_mem_stalkIdeal ⟨V, hV⟩ hxV hmem

/-- The quotient `𝒪_{X,x}/I_x`. -/
noncomputable def subschemeStalk (x : X) : CommRingCat.{u} :=
  CommRingCat.of ((X.presheaf.stalk x) ⧸ I.stalkIdeal x)

/-- The projection `𝒪_{X,x} ⟶ 𝒪_{X,x}/I_x`. -/
noncomputable def toSubschemeStalk (x : X) : X.presheaf.stalk x ⟶ I.subschemeStalk x :=
  CommRingCat.ofHom (Ideal.Quotient.mk (I.stalkIdeal x))

/-- **`𝒪_{V(I),z} = 𝒪_{X,x}/I_x`.** The canonical ring isomorphism, obtained from
`ker_stalkMap_subschemeι` together with the surjectivity of the stalk map of the preimmersion
`I.subschemeι`. -/
noncomputable def subschemeStalkRingEquiv (z : I.subscheme) :
    I.subschemeStalk (I.subschemeι z) ≃+* I.subscheme.presheaf.stalk z :=
  (Ideal.quotEquivOfEq (I.ker_stalkMap_subschemeι z).symm).trans
    (RingHom.quotientKerEquivOfSurjective ((I.subschemeι).stalkMap_surjective z))

/-- The comparison isomorphism is the canonical one: it carries the projection
`𝒪_{X,x} ⟶ 𝒪_{X,x}/I_x` to the stalk map of `I.subschemeι`. -/
@[simp]
theorem subschemeStalkRingEquiv_toSubschemeStalk (z : I.subscheme)
    (a : X.presheaf.stalk (I.subschemeι z)) :
    I.subschemeStalkRingEquiv z ((I.toSubschemeStalk (I.subschemeι z)).hom a)
      = ((I.subschemeι).stalkMap z).hom a := rfl

end AlgebraicGeometry.Scheme.IdealSheafData

namespace Stacks.FibreStalk

/-- **The associated-prime bridge at the stalk of a closed subscheme.** For `z` a point of
`I.subscheme` with image `x` in `X` and `M` a module over `𝒪_{V(I),z}` which is also a module
over `𝒪_{X,x}` through the stalk map, `𝔪_{V(I),z}` is an associated prime of `M` over
`𝒪_{V(I),z}` exactly when `𝔪_{X,x}` is an associated prime of `M` over `𝒪_{X,x}`.

This is the companion of `mem_associatedPrimes_maximalIdeal_stalkMap_fiberι_iff` for the other
preimmersion appearing in Stacks 0GSI, the inclusion of the reduced closure of a point. -/
theorem mem_associatedPrimes_maximalIdeal_stalkMap_subschemeι_iff {X : Scheme.{u}}
    (I : X.IdealSheafData) (z : I.subscheme)
    {M : Type*} [AddCommGroup M] [Module (X.presheaf.stalk (I.subschemeι z)) M]
    [Module (I.subscheme.presheaf.stalk z) M]
    (hsmul : ∀ (b : X.presheaf.stalk (I.subschemeι z)) (m : M),
      b • m = ((I.subschemeι).stalkMap z).hom b • m) :
    maximalIdeal (X.presheaf.stalk (I.subschemeι z))
        ∈ associatedPrimes (X.presheaf.stalk (I.subschemeι z)) M ↔
      maximalIdeal (I.subscheme.presheaf.stalk z)
        ∈ associatedPrimes (I.subscheme.presheaf.stalk z) M :=
  mem_associatedPrimes_maximalIdeal_iff ((I.subschemeι).stalkMap z).hom
    ((I.subschemeι).stalkMap_surjective z) hsmul

end Stacks.FibreStalk
